A||||37||Turbo88||Turbo v6 vs N/A v8.. sorta||||1||
Z||000000||Turbo88||09-18-2001||12:37 AM||||I posted this in another thread but I thought it might be more approprate here. There was a bash archie session going on and I added my thoughts. But this is a better forum for that discussion. I will leave it at this.. (btw, I don't know Archie, have never used any of Archie's products, and have no personal bias against anyone in any of these discussions. The below is not an engine dynamics book, it's just some thoughts on the matter). Enjoy..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Perhaps I might be able to help in this. There is an old saying, 'there is no replacement for displacement'. Well that isn't totally true. Displacement is a measure of how much air an engine can move at a given RPM. That number is known as volumetric efficiency. Volumetric Efficiency (V/E) tells you how much air your engine will flow in the real world vs how much it should flow if it were 100% efficient. The division of those numbers gives you your V/E. For example, the 2.8lt V6 in a Fiero flows aprox 300.3 cubic feet of air per minute if it were 100% efficient. The engine flows between 230 and 250 cfm with makes it about 80% efficient. The lower efficiency is also why you have vacuum (if the engine could pull all the air it has the capacity for, you would much less vacuum). Now, the LS1 engine in my 1999 Trans/Am displaced 346cid. At 100% efficient, it should flow about 602.5 cfm at 6000rpm. Given an hp rating of 310hp, that would suggest a V/E of about 71.9%. At that V/E it flows aprox 433.2 cfm. <BR>Now here is the replacement for displacement. If I put a turbo on that 2.8lt v6 that flows an that allows the engine to flow 433.2 cfm and I add the fuel management to maintain the proper fuel to air ratio, I should be able to make just as much power right? Well sort of. There is one more catch. Because I have the same amount of fuel/air detonating in 1/2 the space, I am going to create more cylinder pressure (hence the reason for high compression). And because of the smaller displacement, it will maintain higher cylinder pressure for a longer period of time (the same reason a higher rod ratio is beneficial). So, for the same amount of fuel and air, a 2.8 will make more power than a 5.7. Here is the catch. Because of the above, the internal parts will be stressed harder but here is the .22, because the parts are smaller, there for have a higher overall density they can withstand a little more (imagine karate-chopping a brand new pencil in 1/2. Easy right? now try to chop one of those 1/2s in 1/2. Same thing). <P>Because the turbo has added more air and increased the V/E over 100% (flowing more air than it has displacement) you have effectively made it a larger engine because you are displacing more air. Now that we understand why this works, please allow me to move into the practical. <P>Obviously, by my name, I am turbo bias. But everything I have quoted above is simple math. Yes, turbos heat the air when compressing it making it less dense. That hurts V/E. Intercoolers help this problem. the 2.8 may not be able to handle the additional stresses of excessive combustion pressures. But they can be built to do so. I personally am building my turbo kit to give me wonderful top end power. I believe the torque of a v8 is to much for the fiero clutch. One person said something about 2 clutches in 4 years. Ouch. BUT (before I get flamed), a top-endy motor won't lay your ass back in the seat from the line, but it will down the track. That same force that puts your ass back in the seat is a pushing that little clutch very hard. And is it doing so right at the same time that it is attempting to mate and accelerate with the flywheel. My power doesn't hit super hard until the clutch is already fully engaged thus reducing torque shock. Do I think that a v8 system is bad? Well, no. It rocks. It's fun. My ls1 t/a runs in the mid 13s stock and it is 1000lbs heavier than my formula (if not more). To put that power plant in my car would be incredible. But, for reasons above, I think I can get to the end of the track quicker and more reliable with a turbo v6. <P>Now someone will most likely mention that everything I have said about the v6 can also be done to a v8 with much more power. Yes this is true. Again we will be making that 346 flow as much air as a big block could on it's own. But that’s not the point. Dollar for Dollar, I have found my money best spent on the turbo v6 because most the parts are already there. No, you can't run 15 lbs of boost on the stock bottom end. But you can on an upgraded one.<P>So ultimately, it just depends on what you want and what you are willing to sacrifice.<P>/rant<P>-Bryan<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||09-18-2001||03:25 AM||||Most of your points are true, to a point. (grin!)<P>Really, to compare two engines, just look at their torque curves super-imposed on the same scale.<P>eg northstar(red) vs LS1(black):<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/torque.jpg"> <P>Or much much better yet, just drive fieros with either engine, and pick the one that planys the biggest grin on your face   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have to say though, that I would never consider a turbo 2.8.(due to engine oiling problems N/A)  The 3.4TDC on the other hand.....<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">   <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||lou_dias||09-18-2001||09:23 AM||||If you divide by cubic inches, the Northstar wins.  Anyway I thought the comparison was supposed to be between a turboed v6 vs. a naturally aspirated v8...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Turbo88||09-18-2001||10:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>If you divide by cubic inches, the Northstar wins.  Anyway I thought the comparison was supposed to be between a turboed v6 vs. a naturally aspirated v8...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It is. And I have to agree, the second best powerplant/drivetrain for the fiero is the northstar. The best, IMHO, is a TH425 mated to a V8 and mounted backwards (pullies facing the rear). I say the Northstar is 2nd best because they are cost prohibitive and don't have a huge aftermarket (like the small block chevy). The 60* motor doesn't either but, you have to admit, most owners of northstar powered cars are not modifying them. I personally love the northstar engine. However, here is something new for everyone to chew on. Chevy is considering the production of a transversly mounted LS1 in an Impala. I was grinning ear to ear when I read this article. Yes ladies and gentlemen, a factory built sideways sitting LS1 that just begs to be put in the back of america's only sports car. <P><BR>Ben, you mentioned a turbo oiling problem? It's not really a problem if it's implemented correctly <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Teh problem you might be thinking of is called 'coking'. It is the process where oil is burned onto the bearings of a turbo and they collect the varnish residue and eventually siez. This can happen very rapidly or over time. Turbo timers help prevent this by allowing the turbo to spool down and cool off. You don't really NEED a turbo timer, you can sit with your car for a minute or so at idle and allow it to spool down. All it does is keep oil pressure on the turbo until the turbo is spinning at the slowest speed and has the lowest heat possible. A lot of people with turbos might come halling ass across a parking lot then shut the engine down with the turbo's bearing running at 400*F and spinning at 120k rpm.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||09-18-2001||10:51 AM||||a TH425 Transmission??? Boat anchor<P>Weeeeeewwwww..<P>What is the purpose of this car?? Lets define a few things here. <P>You won't be seeing an Ls1 FWD impala anytime soon so don't hold your breath. The project , along with the LS6 F body was canned last year, and with the current state of the auto industry (downturn) you won't be seeing it anytime soon. this from the mouths of friends at GM where i used to work in marketing...<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000005||DrDave||09-18-2001||11:05 AM||||The difference is point of view.  I used to BUILD small engines just to SHOW guys what small engines would do.  (Yes you Can make a slant six go. Trust me.)  How ever normaly (not counting nitros) for the same amount of money ya spend on the little engine,  you could REALLY make the larger one go.<BR>    It is truly a matter of taste, do what YOU want to do.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jimmy||09-18-2001||12:56 PM||||Haven't seen to many turbos that run under 13.5 let alone under 14 in the quarter mile.  I know there are some out there but they are rare exceptions. <P>My small block setup beats cars in the very low 13s constantly, and thats slow for a V8 Fiero setup.  Know of quite a few V8s that run 12s 11s locally and even one that runs 10s(archie's site).  I know my motor will last way over 100,000 miles without a rebuild.  Try that with a turbo Fiero!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Turbos are awesome setups, just not on a stock 2.8.  The price it takes to buy the kit is the same amount of money I spent on my v8 swap(including motor, clutch new rad, electric waterpump!).  I can also upgrade my V8 with just about any kind of aftermarket part imaginable and not have to worry about reliability.  In fact I am going to swap in some higher flowing heads/cam to match and headers this winter.  That should net me about what the turbo does on a 2.8 at a third of the price.<P>Lastly a Small block swap is more drivable than a boosted 2.8.  I have over 300 ftlbs of torque on tap from about 1500 RPM to 5000RPM in my current setup.  Way easier to drive than any Fiero I have ever driven.  This was a pleasant surprise when I finished my V8 Fiero, I thought it was going to be uncivil but it was just the opisite.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Turbo88||09-18-2001||01:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jimmy:<BR><B>Haven't seen to many turbos that run under 13.5 let alone under 14 in the quarter mile.  I know there are some out there but they are rare exceptions. <P>My small block setup beats cars in the very low 13s constantly, and thats slow for a V8 Fiero setup.  Know of quite a few V8s that run 12s 11s locally and even one that runs 10s(archie's site).  I know my motor will last way over 100,000 miles without a rebuild.  Try that with a turbo Fiero!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Turbos are awesome setups, just not on a stock 2.8.  The price it takes to buy the kit is the same amount of money I spent on my v8 swap(including motor, clutch new rad, electric waterpump!).  I can also upgrade my V8 with just about any kind of aftermarket part imaginable and not have to worry about reliability.  In fact I am going to swap in some higher flowing heads/cam to match and headers this winter.  That should net me about what the turbo does on a 2.8 at a third of the price.<P>Lastly a Small block swap is more drivable than a boosted 2.8.  I have over 300 ftlbs of torque on tap from about 1500 RPM to 5000RPM in my current setup.  Way easier to drive than any Fiero I have ever driven.  This was a pleasant surprise when I finished my V8 Fiero, I thought it was going to be uncivil but it was just the opisite.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree. The only reason have spent so much time looking into the v6 is everyone who is really fast in the fiero world are v8s. I want to do something different and I think I can do it cheaper. As far as 100k on a turbo motor? I'll take that pepsi challange anyday. And while I'm doing it, I'll ask you to maintain your clutch as long as I can with your small block pushing it. You said something about all the mods you have done to your engine. I'm not going to start a bench racing session. I'll just say this. I will keep all the reciepts for my build on a 60* and you do the same for your V8. Build your motor this winter and I will work on mine as well. next spring, I will meet you in St Louis, MO (you have to drive the car down, not tow it) and we will get together and swap some gear oil. <P>Archie, you are in Chicago, thats about 4 hours from St Louis (O'hare to the border). I say we all get together and have a fiero power fest <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. St Louis is centrally located and has plenty of night life to support the bench racing that will surely occur.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Whaddya think? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||09-18-2001||01:42 PM||||Turbo88... there still is no substitute for displacement- turbo or none.  But it all depends on what you're trying to accomplish with your car.  I'm running a high-output 3.1L V6.  It's nice, but I'd gladly go higher.  I would not bother to put a turbo on, especially on a stock Fiero 2.8L.  There are oiling issues inherent in the design. A 3.1L or 3.4L block fixes those issues at least. Still, I'd rather run a SC 3800 or a SBC.  Anyway- turbos are cool, but especially at high PSi tend to destroy an engine quickly.  I was witness to a turbo-caused blown motor- a local Fiero club member video taped his blown 2.8 (mild) turbo for our entertainment.  Ugh.  <P>But as always, if it adds more power- and you can do it- go for it.  I'm all for whatever "toots your horn"!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  No flame intended either- just my opinion.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jimmy||09-18-2001||02:13 PM||||Sounds like a good natured challenge!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Yeah it would be alot of fun if we could get fieros together for some sort of racing/get together.  <P>I guess what I'm trying to say is the amount of money you'll spend on a 2.8 turbo to get it as powerfull and reliable as a v8 is A LOT more than a 300 hp 340 ftbl V8.  If you don't care about reliability and you just want speed a turbo can make insane power(so can nitrous 1fast2m6  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ), but of course it shortens the life of the motor.<P>BTW I really don't have much for mods on my V8 basically a stock rebuild with a higher compression and a torquer cam.  I am slowest V8 fiero I know and I have no problems taking stock f-bodies stangs and lightnings.  My clutch(centerforce DF) gave me no problems the other night when I went street racing for over 2 hours.  I must have had at least 12 runs and a few 3000 RPM drop the clutch burnouts.  My only problem is the shortened life of my tires.<P>BTW, this thread has been pretty clean, I hope it stays this way.  We don't have to resort to name calling like some other threads I hope.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Jim<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||Joe Torma||09-18-2001||02:47 PM||||Fiero Power Fest?!  Cool idea.  Maybe make it a weekend event?!<P>Gotta have drags, and an autocross.<P>My 3400 is just itching to gobble up some V8.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I left the other nasty threads alone...and I am not starting anything here...opinion below.<P>My only problem with the SBC is that thing sits way too far on the Pass side.  I don't care where you stick your battery or if you eat 10 hamburgers before you race, that offset weight has to screw up the handling.  <P>Drags are another story...torque wins.  <BR>I guess it IS much "cooler" to say you have an 8cly in your car though.<P>Less weight, more power...in my case went with a 3400(300hp says the Desktop Dyno)<P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">    <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000011||jelly2m8||09-18-2001||03:03 PM||||I stand with Jimmy in this arguement. Plus I'll tell turbo boy right now that my 85 GT 350 TPI is only costing me under 6000.00, thats including the V8 swap,new engine, and the car, brake work etc.<P> I'd like to see how ANY V6 turbo works, when you are crusing in high gears with low revs.  ( I have the Isuzu in mine) and I can drive away from 30 mph in 5th gear, and it still pulls as hard as a V6 in full song and glory. Cruisin at 55 mph, the Throttle is only at 7%. Lets see a V6 do that! When drivin normally ( obeying the law) I get awesome gas mileage in the V8, better than any Fiero I have ever owned.<P> If you want to compare your turbo'd V6's to V8's, go ahead, but PLEASE drive a V8 Fiero first! You guys don't seem to understand what it means to have an engine that makes TORQUE.<P> I don't have a V6 turbo Fiero, but I have drivin plenty of turbo cars.  Excluding the Buick GN,and $30k+ race built turbo engines, I'll take a V8 fiero anyday over a forced induction motor.<P>A guy once told me " these young guys just don't understand, you gotta have V8 power"..................<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||jelly2m8||09-18-2001||03:10 PM||||&gt;&gt;&gt;IMy only problem with the SBC is that thing sits way too far on the Pass side.  I don't care where you stick your battery or if you eat 10 hamburgers before you race, that offset weight has to screw up the handling.&lt;&lt;&lt;  <P> Yes the SBC does defenitely change the handling characteristics, Its different at first. But over time you get used to it, and I'll tell you, I have not oversteered the V8 in a corner yet.<P>  I've went in a few turns " hot" with the V8, faster than I do with V6 fieros which do need some opposite lock to get them around the corner, but amazingly the V8 just honks right around them. It still freaks me out. <P> I've even played with different senerios, where i would let off the gas at different parts in the corner, braking in the corner, but everytime, that ole girl just scoots right around the corners.<p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Turbo88||09-18-2001||03:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>I stand with Jimmy in this arguement. Plus I'll tell turbo boy right now that my 85 GT 350 TPI is only costing me under 6000.00, thats including the V8 swap,new engine, and the car, brake work etc.<P> I'd like to see how ANY V6 turbo works, when you are crusing in high gears with low revs.  ( I have the Isuzu in mine) and I can drive away from 30 mph in 5th gear, and it still pulls as hard as a V6 in full song and glory. Cruisin at 55 mph, the Throttle is only at 7%. Lets see a V6 do that! When drivin normally ( obeying the law) I get awesome gas mileage in the V8, better than any Fiero I have ever owned.<P> If you want to compare your turbo'd V6's to V8's, go ahead, but PLEASE drive a V8 Fiero first! You guys don't seem to understand what it means to have an engine that makes TORQUE.<P> I don't have a V6 turbo Fiero, but I have drivin plenty of turbo cars.  Excluding the Buick GN,and $30k+ race built turbo engines, I'll take a V8 fiero anyday over a forced induction motor.<P>A guy once told me " these young guys just don't understand, you gotta have V8 power"..................</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well there was no name calling until now. But that's cool a good natured challenge is all it takes. And before you start insulting my lack of small block knowledge, allow me to inform you that my daily drive contains the most advanced small block ever sent out of factory production. She ran 13.64 out of the box. <P>Someone said there is no replacement for displacement. Read my first entry. displacement is a measure of airflow at rpm. If you are forcing air into the engine are you not displacing more air? I've explained my point. <P>I am all for a powerfest. I can set it up in St Louis which is kinda in the middle of the country. It is reasonably cheap and it's a fun city. Everyone who is interested post your thoughts and I will work the details. St Louis has a drag strip and I can get with the local SCCA region and see if they would help put something together. <P>Oh and Jelly, I am not calling you Jellyboy so keep things real. If you want to flame, bring your car title with you to this event and we can handle it squarely. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||MinnGreenGT||09-18-2001||03:27 PM||||I'm really liking the "Fiero Power Fest" Idea... I don't have a real HOT motor or anything, but I'll show up as a photographer if I have to!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Anyone want to do some initial planning? If you're really planning on playing hard- find a local track and let's see about "renting" it for a day (entrance/participation fee)! We don't want the local PD giving Fiero owners a bad name 'cause we're out street racing in a huge group!  Anyone want to volunteer their resources?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000015||Turbo88||09-18-2001||03:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>I'm really liking the "Fiero Power Fest" Idea... I don't have a real HOT motor or anything, but I'll show up as a photographer if I have to!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Anyone want to do some initial planning? If you're really planning on playing hard- find a local track and let's see about "renting" it for a day (entrance/participation fee)! We don't want the local PD giving Fiero owners a bad name 'cause we're out street racing in a huge group!  Anyone want to volunteer their resources?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll volenteer <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I want to see if there is interest first. However, given how this idea started, I will say that this has to be friendly and with the best intentions. Grudge matches and any side bets have to be between the parties involved and have no affiliation with the Powerfest event. <P>Also, it doesn't matter if you are the fastest. It just matters that you have a damn good time doing it!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||88formula||09-18-2001||04:30 PM||||I've seen a very mild v8 fiero in action and I will tell you this; I WAS VERY VERY IMPRESSED. What I didn't like though was how the shock tower was cut to allow for the water pump to fit and like someone already said the motor looks like it sits too far to one side. I have never actually driven one though so I can’t say they handle bad or good. <P>I wonder how an all aluminum Small block Chevy motor would affect the handling of the car if at all? Maybe Archie can answer that?<P>I think the best power plant would be one that balances engine weight and power to give you the best of handling and acceleration. T turbocharged 2.8, 3.1, 3.4-liter engine (properly build) does just that.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Archie||09-18-2001||11:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B> Archie, you are in Chicago, thats about 4 hours from St Louis (O'hare to the border). I say we all get together and have a fiero power fest  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Whaddya think?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As long as this thread &/or Event doesn't turn into a flame war you can Count me in baby!<P>I assume this would have to take place in the late Fall or early Spring.<P>I would only ask that you find a date that would not interfere with other events that many of us go to. I'm already committed to most of these & I don't want to miss the Fiero Power-Fest. Please avoid conflicts with these dates... the Daytona show, Carlisle, The Dells show, the annual NIFE meeting at V-8 Archie's, The Hot Rod Magazine Power Tour, Knott's Berry Farm and several other events. Please, Please schedule around these events. <P>You get it set-up and I'll bring several V-8 cars. I've many times thought about some kind of get together for my V-8 customers around the country, it looks like several of them have already expressed interest in this event. At this time, I'd like to offer a $500 reward for the highest finishing Fiero running a V-8 installed with one of my kits. ||||reg||14
