Z||000019||artherd||09-18-2001||11:26 PM||||Mmmmm Fiero Power Fest  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">'<P><BR>The best part is, we'd ALL win!!! (I mean, everyone who shows up basically automatically has a really nice car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Count me in if I finish my swap and can spare the driving time!<P>(oh, I was refering to the 2.8's block oilion problems, not anything inherent in a turbo.  As been said, timers, ceramics, and education  can solve those issues  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000020||Turbo88||09-18-2001||11:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> As long as this thread &/or Event doesn't turn into a flame war you can Count me in baby!<P>I assume this would have to take place in the late Fall or early Spring.<P>I would only ask that you find a date that would not interfere with other events that many of us go to. I'm already committed to most of these & I don't want to miss the Fiero Power-Fest. Please avoid conflicts with these dates... the Daytona show, Carlisle, The Dells show, the annual NIFE meeting at V-8 Archie's, The Hot Rod Magazine Power Tour, Knott's Berry Farm and several other events. Please, Please schedule around these events. <P>You get it set-up and I'll bring several V-8 cars. I've many times thought about some kind of get together for my V-8 customers around the country, it looks like several of them have already expressed interest in this event. At this time, I'd like to offer a $500 reward for the highest finishing Fiero running a V-8 installed with one of my kits. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds great! No it won't turn into a flame war. If it does, I will call the whole thing off. I am thinking next spring is a good idea. I have a lot going on through the fall and it would give us plenty of time to coordinate an event. I am going to the Pontiac Uprising in Arkcity Kansas in October (if this motor is done by then, if not, the LS1 will have to do <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">). <P>Did you V8 guys hear that? Archie is going to front 500 bucks to the fastest V8 car out there. I would like to make the same challenge to all you V6 turbo guys that are running my kit (which will be available next month some time). <P>I think this will be a good rivalry in the ranks and should be a lot of fun!! For those of you (Archie included) that have dates you are already committed too, post them and I will contact the strip and the SCCA guys and lock down something compatible! Sinse this got off topic a little, I am going to make a seperate post about it and see if what i can drum up. <P>This should be a blast and good competition too! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||BlackDragon||09-19-2001||12:09 AM||||hhmmmm, not trying to stir flames, but heres how i see it. turbos are awesome. they are a great way to add power, along with nitro. they are some of the easiest bolt on power additives i have seen, BUT if you take your stock v6 and add a turbo and nitro, and i take my v8 and add a turbo with the same amount of boost, and nitro with the same shot, i would wager just about any amount of cash that i will win given same tires and other car parts. <BR>dont mistake what im saying. turbo v6's are still cool, but you can only get so much power out of a small motor, the more cubes, the more potential. im sure you could even stroke&bore a large v6 to have the same cubes as a v8, but the problem remains that you could also stroke&bore that v8, and you will have more. personaly if i had my choice of motors in a fiero it would be an aluminum 502 BBC TPI without a turbo. maybe, MAYBE a little shot of Nitro. but i realy dont think you would need it.<BR>like i stated above, im not nocking the v6, its a good little motor. but i think if the fiero had stayed in production a little longer, the vette would have become extinct because they would have eventualy put a 350 in the fiero, because they would have realized that it would be a big hit.<P>this is only an oppinion. you can argue all you want, but this will still be MY opinion. i could argue all i want, and you would still have YOUR opinion<P>------------------<BR>WTB:CHEAP black standard fiero. no engine is fine, any model/condition gray interior is a plus. must have title||||reg||1
Z||000022||Wannabe||09-19-2001||12:30 AM||||It's not likely the offset location of the SBC has any significant effect on handling. If you consider that the SBC weighs around 500lb (approx - we're estimating here) and as much as 1/4 of it extends to the passenger side of the car (this must be exaggerated - the figure I've heard is 2") then that adds about 125lbs to the passenger side. Considering that the typical male weighs between 150-200lbs and sits on the driver's side, this should balance things out. Either way, I doubt it would even be noticable without doing a precise corner weighting of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Turbo88||09-19-2001||12:47 AM||||Dragon, <P>    You are correct but that has never been my point. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I have never seen a force induction small block in a Fiero. I am talking dollar for dollar, a V6 turbo will run with a V8 natural. But all of the above is what started the powerfest idea. I see it growing from there. Of course if you used forced in a larger engine you will get better results. But thats not the arguement <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Jimmy||09-19-2001||12:49 AM||||I think I've read a small block weighs about 340 lbs and the 2.8 weighed 260 lbs. <P>With Archie's kit I haven't been able to tell the difference in handling that much.  I keep up just fine with fellow v6 fieros in the twisties.  I have by no means thrown my car around a track or SCCA course which would be a better test than street driving.  I upgraded some suspension parts while I was doing my V8 swap so I know that helped make up for the exrta 100 lbs or so.  I also moved the battery up front.  I have this twisty part of road that is right by my house.  Posted speed is 10 mph, I get thru it faster with my V8 than my stock GT.  I attain this to the poly and lowering springs.<P>The V8 didn't get rid of the ugly wheel gap in the back either.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I am still using stock springs and struts in the rear.  I am going to buy coilovers(350lb springs) and kyb struts soon.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Jim||||reg||1
