Z||000030||Kwez||09-19-2001||06:52 PM||||what about same amount of air for a turbo V6 vs a n/a V8 ... <BR>but how much will the greater number of explosions/sec in the V8 influence the power ?<P>or, compare a n/a 3.5L V8 to a n/a 3.5L V6<P>or am I completely in the woods on this ?<P>'wondering Kwez'||||reg||5
Z||000031||Turbo88||09-19-2001||08:18 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kwez:<BR><B>what about same amount of air for a turbo V6 vs a n/a V8 ... <BR>but how much will the greater number of explosions/sec in the V8 influence the power ?</B><P>Well, nothing really. You will have more explosions but each explosion will be smaller. Because you are, in effect, flowing the air a SBC engine would on it's own. <P>Imagine taking firecracker and putting it under an empty soup can turned upside down and setting it off. It will pop the can well into the air. Now imagine putting the same firecracker under a coffee can and repeating the same experiment. You won't move the coffee can as far. Now imagine putting a smaller firecracker under that coffee can. Please understand that this is a very crude and general analogy but it demonstrates the same principle. Yes the coffee can is heaver (so is a small block piston) but the balancing act it that you have two more explosions per 2 revolutions. <P><B>or, compare a n/a 3.5L V8 to a n/a 3.5L V6</B><P>Well this is a completely different animal than our discussion. A poweradder like a turbo or blower is effectively artifical displacement. Because it is forcing your motor to flow the air of a larger motor without a poweradder. <P>On an engine with the same profile, turning the same RPM, I would have to say the v8 would be stronger because it will be more volumetricly effiecent (two more intake runners, two more sets of valves, ect.). But that is only because it gives you more room to move the air. A turbo negates this because it is forcing air into your engine. <P>But here is a catch. Lets say this 3.5lt engine is given certian overall parameters that you must adhear too. For example, say you are only allows a total of a given amount of intake runner size or combustion chamber area and you have to divide that amount out by the number of cylinders you have (if you can only have 300cc of combustion chamber area, you will have smaller combustion chambers on the v8 but no more compression because there is less A/F charge in the cylinder). These considerations are very real world thoughts crossing the minds of engineers when the build engines.<P><B>or am I completely in the woods on this ?<P>'wondering Kwez'</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I don't think you are in the woods  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> These are very good questions!<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||Tina||09-19-2001||09:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> At this time, I'd like to offer a $500 reward for the highest finishing Fiero running a V-8 installed with one of my kits. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>So...... Archie, that silver one of your with the dual carburators is going to be attending?<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000033||pureSKID||11-07-2001||08:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B><BR>Drags are another story...torque wins.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree with that statement.  It's all in lbs/power.  More power + less weight = faster.  I think in a drag race, hp is a lot more important than torque.  For instance... 350hp/250tq or 250hp/350tq in the exact same car - the one with more torque definitely will not be the winner.  Especially low end torque for a drag... that means **** all cuz you never see the lower 1/2 of your rpm range anway.  On the street in your normal car however, you gotta have some low end torque...and it's more fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||FieroRacer||11-07-2001||09:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>Turbo88... there still is no substitute for displacement- turbo or none.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm planning on building a 320ci V6 for Demon!!!  How's that for "Small Displacement" V6's?||||reg||1
Z||000035||rgeeinc||11-09-2001||05:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR>[B]I stand with Jimmy in this argument. Plus I'll tell turbo boy right now that my 85 GT 350 TPI is only costing me under 6000.00, thats including the V8 swap,new engine, and the car, brake work etc.<P> <P> If you want to compare your turbo'd V6's to V8's, go ahead, but PLEASE drive a V8 Fiero first! You guys don't seem to understand what it means to have an engine that makes TORQUE.<P> I don't have a V6 turbo Fiero, but I have drivin plenty of turbo cars.   <BR> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think jelly2m8 has said a lot here but, I don't think Turbo88 was listing, or wanted to pay attention to what was being said. In fact Jimmy made a lot of good comments, a long with a lot of other guys, in this thread.<BR>I would like to comment on just a few thing in this reply.  Dollar for Dollar V6 vs V8 they are both going to cost you a lot of money. Only you can decide which engine you want to build, but don't close your mind to other options, once you have decided on your engine. Having owned both V6 turbo and V8 in my Fiero, I think I am qualified to give a opinion. But won't. The Fiero power fest will tell the story. Anyone wanting to build a V6 turbo (like jelly2m8 said) take a ride in a V8 first, then make your decision.<BR> The guys that are talking about handling, I don't notice much difference at all, from one to the other. Now power, that's another story. My V8 wins hands down over my V6 turbo. The V6 turbo's do make a lot of power and do run good, but in my opinion you can't compare them to a V8 engine.<BR>For the guys that are considering building a faster Fiero, Be it Turbo or V8 I recommend that you save your money until after the power fest. Make sure you show up (even if you don't own a Fiero (YET.) Then make your decision. You will have the cold hard facts, then decide how you want to spend your money. <BR>Given enough notice, I for one am going to try to make it.   <BR>See you there.<P>Bob...||||reg||1
Z||000036||Cutlass207TDC||11-09-2001||12:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B>Perhaps I might be able to help in this. There is an old saying, 'there is no replacement for displacement'. Well that isn't totally true. Displacement is a measure of how much air an engine can move at a given RPM. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>First you say that the saying "There is no replacement for displacement" is wrong.  Then u say by adding a turbo, you increase the amount of air therefore increasing displacement is good.  Looks like something went wrong here.<P><BR>||||reg||13
Z||000037||Cutlass207TDC||11-09-2001||12:42 PM||||What if someone is running like 16 pounds of boost on a completely blueprinted v6.  I think that can take most v8's.  i know of some 4 cyl.'s with turbos running like 450hp.  Then again a v8 can also do the same if not more.  There are a lot of other factors one must look into.  Tranny, suspension, weight of car, tires.  I'm kind of in between a turbo v6 or NA v8.  I would have to drive both to choose just 1.  The best solution here is obvious.  Drop a lingenfelter twin turbo corvette v8 into a fiero. 650HP  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
